closed-term pool: 12 terms (size <= 4); roots: {'0': 1, 'S': 5, 'add': 3, 'mul': 3}

(1) anchor <=> (R)&(D):  lgg(data) == phi(z..) up to renaming
  x+0=x  (x twice)             schema eq(add(z0,0),z0)             data sets    286  agree    286  recovering    258
  x+x=x*x (x four times)       schema eq(add(z0,z0),mul(z0,z0))    data sets    286  agree    286  recovering    258
  x+S0=Sx (closed S0 in phi)   schema eq(add(z0,S(0)),S(z0))       data sets    286  agree    286  recovering    258
  x=0 & Ax(x=x)  (bound x)     schema and(eq(z0,0),all(x,eq(x,x))) data sets    286  agree    286  recovering    258
  x+y=y+x (two vars)           schema eq(add(z0,z1),add(z1,z0))    data sets  70296  agree  70296  recovering  58330
  x*0=0 & y=y (two vars)       schema and(eq(mul(z0,0),0),eq(z1,z1)) data sets  70296  agree  70296  recovering  58330

    examples of non-anchors (sound specialisations):
     data terms ['S(0)', 'S(S(0))'] -> lgg eq(add(S(z0),0),S(z0))
     data terms ['add(0,0)', 'add(S(0),0)'] -> lgg eq(add(add(z0,0),0),add(z0,0))
     x+y=y+x on diagonal data ['0', 'S(0)', 'add(0,0)'] -> lgg eq(add(z0,z0),add(z0,z0))
     x+S0=Sx with data terms S0 and 0 (S0 also a closed subterm of phi) -> lgg eq(add(z0,S(0)),S(z0))
     x+S0=Sx with data terms S0 and SS0 (root S only) -> lgg eq(add(S(z0),S(0)),S(S(z0)))

(2) instance-set injectivity: two instances with distinct roots per variable and distinct values
  lgg of the two generic instances == schema for all phi: True

(3) open-term data, named encoding, guards {closed(z), free-for(z)} learned as the strongest guards true on all data
  data terms ['0', 'S(0)']  lgg eq(add(0,z0),z0)  learned guards {'closed(z0)': True}
     accept 0+t=t for t = S(S(0))      : True
     accept 0+t=t for t = p2           : False
     accept 0+t=t for t = mul(p1,p2)   : False
  data terms ['0', 'S(0)', 'add(p1,S(0))']  lgg eq(add(0,z0),z0)  learned guards {'closed(z0)': False}
     accept 0+t=t for t = S(S(0))      : True
     accept 0+t=t for t = p2           : True
     accept 0+t=t for t = mul(p1,p2)   : True
  (accepting t = p2, a parameter, means accepting the universal closure  Ap2 (0+p2=p2),  i.e.  Ax(0+x=x).)

